Rattle-Decay Minimal

Ingredients: MEXICAN QUIJADA / JAWBONE RATTLE + MINIMAL TECHNO + PREPARED WURLITZER + CLAY UDU + BASS FLUTE + SHORT ANALOG SUB

Creative Catalyst: The genre began during a dry-season residency inside a retired salt-packing depot near Puerto Escondido, where percussionist Lucía Barragán was recording several quijadas whose loose teeth rattled for different lengths after each strike while Berlin producer Erik Voss programmed stripped 124 BPM minimal techno in the former dispatch office. The breakthrough came when Erik stopped sampling the quijada as a one-shot shaker. Lucía struck the bone once and let the loose teeth continue chattering naturally until the resonance died. Each instrument produced a different decay contour: some stopped almost immediately, others unraveled through several irregular pulses. Erik began composing bars around the entire decay rather than only the initial hit. Prepared Wurlitzer, clay udu, bass flute, and tightly enveloped analog sub were added to create a form where one acoustic strike contains its own miniature rhythm after the hand has already stopped moving.

Rattle-Decay Minimal is a dry acoustic-electronic club form built around post-attack rhythm. Minimal techno supplies the chassis: compact four-on-the-floor kick, narrow hats, muted chord cells, restrained sub, gradual filter movement, long repetition, and small structural changes that become significant because the arrangement remains sparse. The Mexican quijada supplies the opposing logic through a hard initial impact followed by a loose cascade of tooth chatter whose internal timing cannot be perfectly quantized.

The quijada is recorded with one close condenser aimed across the tooth line, one ribbon microphone near the lower jaw body, one removable contact microphone fixed non-destructively near the ramus, and one restrained room microphone. The condenser captures the initial slap, tooth clicks, shell-like chatter, and short high-frequency bursts. The ribbon provides hollow bone resonance and lower body. The contact channel isolates mechanical vibration after the visible strike has ended. The room microphone remains low enough that the full acoustic decay can be heard without becoming reverberant wash.

Players use palm strikes, knuckle taps, stick scrapes, short rubs across the tooth row, damped hits, suspended hand-held strikes, and controlled choke gestures. Continuous shaking is avoided. The defining sound is a single deliberate impact followed by an uncontrolled but repeatable-enough cascade of loose mechanical clicks.

The central compositional device is the After-Rattle Window. Every important quijada event is divided into four perceptual zones: IMPACT, CHATTER, THINNING, CLEAR. IMPACT is the initial hand or stick contact. CHATTER is the dense early tooth response. THINNING is the irregular final sequence of isolated clicks. CLEAR is the silence after the last tooth stops. Electronic parts are arranged around these zones without using sensors or automatic onset detection.

A techno kick may continue through IMPACT but disappear during THINNING so the smallest final tooth clicks become audible. In another section, the kick and sub may be absent through the entire CHATTER phase and return only when CLEAR begins. The electronic grid remains exact, while the acoustic decay floats inside it.

The most important rule is Decay Ownership: once a quijada is struck, no decorative percussion may be added merely to imitate or reinforce its internal rattle. The natural decay is treated as a complete rhythmic phrase. If the teeth chatter for three-quarters of a bar, that space belongs to the instrument.

Minimal-techno drums remain compact and severe. Kick is short, centered, and usually four-on-the-floor, though selected beats may be muted to expose THINNING. Closed hats use narrow eighths or sparse sixteenths. Claps are rare and dry. Open hats, risers, and large cymbal tails are strongly restricted because they mask the acoustic decay.

The analog sub is mono, tightly enveloped, and used as pressure rather than bass melody. Notes favor root, fifth, octave drop, and occasional semitone approach. Sub frequently enters after CLEAR instead of on the initial quijada strike, making silence function as a hinge between acoustic chatter and electronic weight.

Prepared Wurlitzer supplies short middle-register harmony. Felt, paper, removable damping, and hand-muted tine technique produce clipped minor sevenths, suspended seconds, bare fifths, octave dyads, and narrow clusters. The Wurlitzer rarely sustains. Its attacks are placed either immediately before IMPACT, creating tension, or after CLEAR, creating a clean harmonic reset.

Clay udu provides low acoustic cavity percussion. Palm closures, open-hole tones, muted shell taps, and short bass pops are used sparingly. The udu never doubles every kick. Instead, it replaces occasional machine hits and provides a rounded air cavity contrast to the quijada's dry mechanical chatter.

Bass flute occupies the low-mid air register. It plays breath-heavy fundamentals, soft octave drops, key noise, narrow descending seconds, tongue-stop attacks, and low airy pedals. Its entrances are timed primarily during CLEAR windows, when the quijada has fully stopped and the arrangement can support a continuous tone without masking mechanical detail.

Vocals are optional and concise. Dry spoken Spanish or English fragments may describe crate numbers, salt weights, dispatch times, tooth counts, impact marks, inspection notes, and simple procedural commands. Conventional sung choruses are unnecessary. Voice should behave like another short event, not a continuous topline.

Effects are tightly limited. The main quijada signal remains almost completely dry. A single-repeat mono delay may be applied to selected IMPACT events, but the delayed copy must end before the natural THINNING phase becomes indistinguishable. Wurlitzer may use a short dark plate. Bass flute gets a restrained chamber. Udu remains close and dry. Sub is effect-free.

The defining tension is deterministic machine time versus acoustic after-motion. The hand strikes once; the instrument keeps producing rhythm after the human action has already ended.

Genre DNA

Tempo & Pulse: 120–126 BPM, primarily 4/4 minimal techno with compact quarter-note kick, narrow hats, rare dry claps, controlled mute windows, and acoustic quijada decays that spill irregularly across subdivisions without quantization.

Harmonic Palette: Dorian and Aeolian centers, open fifths, minor sevenths, suspended 2nds, octaves, narrow semitone clusters, pedal tones, and long static harmonic zones where rhythmic decay carries more development than chord movement.

Production Signatures:

1. After-Rattle Window — major quijada events move through IMPACT → CHATTER → THINNING → CLEAR, with electronic density shaped around the full decay rather than only the strike.
2. Decay Ownership — once the quijada is struck, no shaker, hat fill, or ornamental percussion is allowed to imitate the natural tooth chatter.
3. Clear-Point Sub — selected sub entries occur only after the last audible tooth click, turning the end of acoustic motion into the start of electronic pressure.
4. Thin-Hat Mute — hats or claps are removed during THINNING so isolated late tooth clicks remain audible.
5. Udu Substitution — low clay-drum impacts replace selected machine kicks or percussion events rather than stacking continuously.
6. No-Rattle Loop Rule — the defining quijada decay must come from complete real strikes; short looped shaker slices cannot become the main rhythmic identity.

Dynamics: Intensity grows through harder impacts, longer undamped quijada decays, shorter CLEAR windows, denser kick continuity, brighter Wurlitzer attacks, deeper udu cavity tones, and stronger bass-flute entries. Climaxes remain dry, skeletal, and mechanically detailed rather than maximal.

Scene & Origin Lore

Rattle-Decay Minimal formed inside a decommissioned coastal salt-packing and freight depot near Puerto Escondido. The building retained white mineral dust, timber loading tables, galvanized scales, faded blue shipping numbers, burlap sacks, rusted hand trucks, enamel inspection trays, narrow vents, and a hard concrete floor that exaggerated every small mechanical click.

Quijada player Lucía Barragán had been comparing several instruments built from naturally dried jawbones with different tooth looseness. She noticed that two apparently identical strikes could produce very different after-rattles depending on where the bone was held and how much the jaw flexed. One instrument stopped within a fraction of a second; another produced a chain of late isolated clicks long after the initial slap.

Producer Erik Voss was working in the dispatch office on severe minimal-techno sketches. His first attempt sampled one quijada hit and placed it on every second beat like a shaker. The result sounded generic. He removed the loop, recorded one full strike from impact to silence, and muted the hi-hat until the last tooth click disappeared. The resulting negative space felt more rhythmic than the programmed percussion he had deleted.

Prepared-Wurlitzer player Daniela Cruz joined after designing short damped chords that could frame the acoustic event without overlapping its decay. Udu player Mateo Salgado added low cavity hits only where machine kicks were deliberately removed. Bass-flutist Clara Weiss completed the ensemble with breath-heavy sustained notes that entered after CLEAR points and disappeared before the next major strike.

Their first performances were called decay audits. A simple board beside the stage showed four columns labeled IMPACT, CHATTER, THINNING, CLEAR. Nothing was automated from the chart. It existed only to remind the audience that the important rhythm continued after the visible hand motion stopped.

The visual language developed around salt white, faded freight blue, rust orange, bone ivory, graphite black, burlap brown, dull aluminum, and weak amber loading lamps. The unofficial rule is that the last tooth click deserves as much space as the first kick.

